  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 225 AM MDT Sat Sep 19 2026\n\nNo major changes. Please see previous Long Term discussion...\n\nForecast for next week looks to remain largely the same from day to \nday under weak pressure gradients aloft with a few chances for some \nscattered precipitation, but otherwise a cool start to the weak with \na slow and steady warming trend through the remainder of. Upper \nlevel pattern will consist of weak ridging trying to build in on \nMonday, but this will be overtaken by a weak low lifting up from \nCalifornia and slowly progressing across the region through the end \nof the forecast period. At the surface, Monday will see our forecast \nregion in a post-frontal environment, with a weak boundary then \nsitting across the WY/NE border area and then lifting east-northeast \nas a warm front into the later days of the week. Under this pattern \nMonday will be the coolest day of the forecast period as widespread \nhighs generally sit in the 60's to right around 70 in the warmest \nspots. Increasing warmth then expected as warmer air from the \nsouthwest moves across the region, but don't expect a crazy warmup \nas we increase only 1-3 degrees each day, keeping the region mostly \nin the 70's through the week until Thursday when a few more 80's may \nfinally begin popping up. A brief blip of moisture may produce \nincreased chances of showers and storms on Tuesday as the California \nlow lifts up, but without a stout forcing mechanism don't expect \nmuch outside of general showers and thunderstorms and coverage \nisolated to scattered at best. Otherwise, a few isolated showers or \nstorms possible each day as well with nothing impactful expected.",
